  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThe surface low mentioned in the last several discussions has \nfinally lifted out of the northeastern Gulf, allowing for the return \nof onshore flow and moisture to the area today and tonight. Another \ncold front will move across South Texas Sunday. Low-level moisture \nconvergence and forcing along the front will lead to convection \nSunday. With CAPE values expected to be nearly 2000 J/kg, and bulk \nshear around 45-50 knots, a few thunderstorms could be strong to \nsevere. SPC has included the southern portion of our area in a \nmarginal risk (level 1 of 5) for severe thunderstorms, with the \nprimary risk being large hail. \n\nBehind the cold front, cool and dry conditions will stream in with \nnorth to northeasterly winds. A secondary front will then push \nthrough on Tuesday, ensuring a chilly Thanksgiving across the area. \nHigh temperatures will be in the upper 50s to low 60s!\n\nWith continued long period swells, higher than normal tides, and the \nreturn of northeasterly flow, minor coastal flooding is likely to \nremain a threat through early next week. As such, have extended the \nCoastal Flood Advisory through Monday morning, understanding that we \ncould need to extend this further in time.",
